TY  - JOUR
AU  - Austin, Jennifer L.; Agar, Graydon
TI  - Helping Young Children Follow Their Teachers' Directions: The Utility of High Probability Command Sequences in Pre-K and Kindergarten Classrooms.
PY  - 2005
AB  - Abstract Compliance with adult instructions is crucial to children's academic and social success. One strategy that has proven particularly successful in increasing compliance is the use of a high probability command sequence (HPCS) prior to delivering a low probability request. However, most studies conducted in school settings have focused on the procedure's applicability to students with disabilities. In this study, we targeted typically developing pre-k and kindergarten students with a history of noncompliance to teacher directives. We taught the teachers to integrate the HPCS into ongoing classroom activities as a mechanism for increasing student compliance. For three of the four participants, clear increases in compliance to low probability requests were observed. Interestingly, however, compliant responding to low probability requests were not always preceded by compliance to high probability requests. KEY WORDS: behavioral momentum, high probability command sequence, compliance, classroom management, stimulus control ********** The ability to consistently follow teacher instructions is a crucial skill for children who are to be successful in school. Noncompliance inevitably leads to fewer educational opportunities for students, because they are less likely to engage in important academic and social behaviors requested by their teachers. Additionally, the noncompliance of even a small number of children in a classroom may affect the educational environment of the entire class by taking valuable teacher time and resources to manage the problems. Unfortunately, many teachers are ill-equipped to deal with such problems, and frequently resort to coercive strategies to manage the behavior of noncompliant children (Latham, 2002). Coercion, while commonly accepted in schools as an effective means of control, is more likely than not to produce unwanted behavioral side effects (Sidman, 1989). Therefore, it is imperative that teachers learn non-aversive means for managing behavior problems such as noncompliance. The use of such methods has important implications for the success of individual children and the effectiveness of learning environments for all students in a class. One positive strategy that has proven beneficial in increasing compliance is the use of high-probability command sequences (HPCS). The HPCS, also referred to as "behavioral momentum" (Mace et al., 1988; Nevin, Mandell, & Atak, 1983) involves the presentation of a sequence of requests to which a person is likely to comply (i.e., high-probability requests) followed by a request for a behavior with a lower likelihood of compliance (i.e., low-probability request). Using the HPCS prior to presenting the low-probability request has been shown to increase the likelihood of compliance to requests that have historically resulted in noncompliance (Ardoin, Martens, & Wolf, 1999; Davis, Brady, Hamilton, McEvoy, & Williams, 1994; Davis, Brady, Williams, & Hamiltion, 1992; Davis & Reichle, 1996; Ducharme & Worling, 1994; Houlihan, Jacobson, & Brandon, 1994; Kennedy, Itkonen, & Lindquist, 1995; Killu, Sainato, Davis, Ospelt, & Paul, 1998; Mace & Belfiore, 1990; Mace et al., 1988; McComas, Wacker, & Cooper, 1998; Romano & Roll, 2000; Rortverdt & Miltenberger, 1994; Zarcone, Iwata, Mazaleski, & Smith, 1994). Implementation of HPCS also has been shown to reduce the latency to initiate compliant responding (Ardoin et al., 1999; Belfiore, Lee, Vargas, & Skinner, 1997; Wehby & Hollahan, 2000). Several of the studies involving evaluation of the HPCS procedure have been conducted within the context of school settings (Ardoin et al., 1999; Belfiore et al., 1997; Davis et al., 1994; Davis & Reichle, 1996; Houlihan et al., 1994; Kennedy et al., 1995; Killu et al., 1998; Wehby & Hollahan, 2000). In settings outside of education, the populations targeted for validation of HPCS procedures have been predominantly persons with developmental disabilities (see Rortverdt & Miltenberger, 1994 for an exception). …
